# AUTHOR LINE GROUPING — the PAREN × COMMENT cell. A `#` INSIDE the grouping.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture is a deliberately
# UNFORMATTED INPUT — the assertion is on what `gg fmt` turns it INTO
# (`fmt_author_rows_author_paren_holds_a_comment`, exact text). A sweep would
# rewrite it into its own expected output and the cell would go green-vacuous.
#
# AXES CROSSED, and crossing them is the whole point: the ANCHOR axis (an author
# grouping paren spanning a line break, `author_paren_spans_rows.gg`) × the
# COMMENT axis (`interior_comment_without_comma.gg`,
# `trailing_comment_per_row.gg`). Each axis had cells; the PAIR had none, and
# the pair is where the two reads disagree.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IT PINS. `author_line_break_between` anchors past the author's grouping
# parens so that a newline between a node and its own `)` is not read as a break
# between ELEMENTS. A `#` written inside those parens sits BEFORE the `)` — so
# it is before the anchor, and an anchored-only scan reads this row as
# continuing. It must not. The emitter's leading- and trailing-comment hooks
# fire only where a row STARTS, so a row continued past a comment has no hook to
# emit it at, and the comment is re-parented to the NEXT row. The probe
# therefore reads `#` over the UNANCHORED region and `\n` over the anchored one.
#
# The expected output below is BYTE-IDENTICAL to what the pre-change binary
# produces for the comment's placement — this cell asserts that the grouping
# feature did not MOVE a comment, which is the strongest form the claim can take.
#
# RED-verified against the shipped-but-wrong intermediate (Core #12): with the
# probe anchored for BOTH reads, `gg fmt` ABORTED on this file — exit 101,
# `panicked at src/formatter/mod.rs: a continued row holds a '#' between
# elements`. With that debug assertion neutralised (modelling a release build)
# the comment is not dropped but RELOCATED, off `(1)` and onto the following
# row. Both were measured; the shipped probe produces neither.
from std.collections import Vector

void main():
    Vector[int] xs = [(1 # inside the paren
), 2,
        3, 4,
    ]
    Vector[int] ys = [( 5  # spaced, the sibling costume
 ), 6,
        7, 8,
    ]
    print(xs.len() + ys.len())
